From: Austin Burdine <austin@ghost.org>
Subject: [PATCH] [heap] Treat a zero embedder allocation rate as a low allocation rate

Since https://crrev.com/c/5935313 the allocation throughput trackers are
SmoothedBytesAndDuration values with no lower bound. An isolate whose
sampled CppHeap allocation throughput is exactly 0 -- because it has not
allocated there, or because the smoothed value has decayed to 0 after a
long idle period -- has ComputeMutatorUtilizationImpl() map that 0 to a
mutator utilization of 0 ("maximal allocation"). Heap::HasLowAllocationRate()
is then false and the MemoryReducer cannot start a GC through its
low-allocation condition; only the 100s watchdog (or
ShouldOptimizeForMemoryUsage()) can. Observed in Node.js 24/26.

Zero allocation is the lowest possible allocation rate; report it as such.
Exact zero is deliberate: positive throughput keeps the existing comparison
against the embedder GC speed.

(Paths are deps/v8/... for applying to a Node.js tree; use -p3 or rewrite to
src/heap/heap.cc for a V8 checkout.)

--- a/deps/v8/src/heap/heap.cc
+++ b/deps/v8/src/heap/heap.cc
@@ -3716,8 +3716,15 @@ bool Heap::HasLowOldGenerationAllocationRate() {
 }
 
 bool Heap::HasLowEmbedderAllocationRate() {
+  const double embedder_allocation_rate =
+      tracer()->EmbedderAllocationThroughputInBytesPerMillisecond();
+  // A sampled CppHeap allocation throughput of exactly 0 is the lowest
+  // possible allocation rate. ComputeMutatorUtilization() would otherwise
+  // treat a mutator speed of 0 as maximal allocation and the memory reducer
+  // could not start through its low-allocation condition.
+  if (embedder_allocation_rate == 0) return true;
   double mu = ComputeMutatorUtilization(
-      "Embedder", tracer()->EmbedderAllocationThroughputInBytesPerMillisecond(),
+      "Embedder", embedder_allocation_rate,
       tracer()->EmbedderSpeedInBytesPerMillisecond());
   const double kHighMutatorUtilization = 0.993;
   return mu > kHighMutatorUtilization;
